    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Web basics</p>
    <h1>What Is URL Encoding (Percent-Encoding)?</h1>
    <p class="lead">Web addresses can only contain a limited set of characters. URL encoding replaces anything else with a percent sign and two hex digits, so a space becomes %20.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>Why encoding is needed</h2>
<p>A URL uses characters such as ?, &amp;, = and / with a special meaning. If a value you put in a link contains one of those characters, the browser may read it as part of the address. Encoding turns the value into a safe form that can be passed through unchanged.</p>
<h2>How it works</h2>
<p>Each unsafe byte is written as a percent sign followed by its two-digit hex code. A space is %20, an ampersand is %26, and an equals sign is %3D. The receiving site decodes the value back to the original text.</p>
<h2>Which function to use</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Whole address:</strong> encodeURI keeps the separators such as : / ? and &amp;, so the structure of the link survives.</li>
<li><strong>A single value:</strong> encodeURIComponent escapes everything that is not a letter, digit or a few safe marks, so a value containing &amp; or = cannot break the query string.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Plus signs and spaces in forms</h2>
<p>HTML forms often encode spaces as + rather than %20. Both mean a space in query strings, but a plus sign inside a path is taken literally, so check which form the receiving site expects.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/url-encoder.html">URL Encoder and Decoder</a> converts text in both directions. Nothing you type is sent to a server.</p>
      <section class="worked">
        <h2>Worked example</h2>
        <p>The text a b&amp;c becomes a%20b%26c when encoded for a query value. The space becomes %20 and the ampersand becomes %26, so the ampersand is not read as a separator.</p>
      </section>
      <section class="faq-list">
        <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
        <details><summary>Why does a space become %20 in some places and + in others?</summary><p>%20 is always a space in a URL path. In HTML form submissions, + is often used for spaces inside query strings.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Should I use encodeURI or encodeURIComponent?</summary><p>Use encodeURI for a whole address, and encodeURIComponent for a single value that goes inside it.</p></details>
        <details><summary>What is double encoding?</summary><p>Encoding text that is already encoded turns %20 into %2520. Encode once, at the point where the value is added to the URL.</p></details>
      </section>
